in chimera you can animate structure  translations and rotations in xyz  changes in the representation of ribbons  and atoms changes in opacity and color  and more in this tutorial we will  explore some of these capabilities by  creating a short animation with the  hemoglobin structure 4 hhb  before we begin to animate it is  important to obtain the correct  dimensions for the graphics window if  you're uploading to youtube you'll want  your dimensions to be of the ratio 1280  by 720. to set this ratio for the  graphics window type into the command  line window size all one word space 1280  space 720 and press enter  now this is a relatively small window to  work in so feel free to scale up the  dimensions if needed for this tutorial  i'll be scaling up by a factor of 1.5  which will make the graphics window have  dimensions of 1920 by 1080  now we can go to tools  utilities and animation to access the  animation interface  we will be using this interface to  create snapshot scenes called keyframes  each containing a different depiction  state of the structure  once we arrange these keyframes in the  timeline chimera will transition  smoothly between them to create the  animation  at this point it's also worth noting  that chimera's animation runs at a rate  of 24 frames per second  this value will become an important  conversion factor later on  to create our first keyframe we'll take  a snapshot of the structure in its  default state using the green plus icon  next we're going to set up a color  change by going to actions  color all options and selectively  coloring the ribbons green  now we'll take a snapshot of the result  to create the second keyframe  it is also possible to update keyframes  once you have created them let's say  that you want to color the protein  ribbon something other than green  to do this we first make the desired  change and then right click on the  corresponding keyframe and select update  now that keyframe contains the change  made in the graphics window  for our final scene we are going to zoom  into a heme group by first selecting  chain b  then changing the selection mode to  intersect  and finally selecting heme  then under actions we can focus in  on the heme group  clear the selection  and take another snapshot to create the  final keyframe  it is now time to begin arranging the  keyframes in the timeline  to do this we drag them in one by one  notice the number that appears at the  bottom as we drag them in  that number denotes the number of frames  currently contained in the animation  recalling that chimera runs at a rate of  24 frames per second we see that the  current duration is about 3 seconds  by right clicking on the keyframe and  selecting properties  we can set the transition length  precisely  this value denotes the number of frames  between the current and preceding  keyframes  now the color change will take exactly  three seconds to complete  similarly we will allow three seconds  for the zoom transition by again going  to properties and then setting the frame  number to 72.  in the timeline the three keyframes are  equally spaced in accordance with our  frame number settings  finally we can add a 360 degree rotation  to the animation by dragging the roll  action into the timeline we don't want  the rotation to be too fast though so we  will allow seven seconds by going to  properties and setting the frame number  to 168.  now we are ready to export the animation  to do this we click the red and grey  record button  now we can give our animation a suitable  name such as 4-hb dash animation  next we need to select the file format  here i'll go with the default h.264.mp4  finally we hit record and wait for the  animation to render  with the export complete we can now view  the finished animation  you
 

